nodecode-telegram-business is a rewrite of NousResearch's NousResearch/hermes-labs-plugins:

  https://github.com/NousResearch/hermes-labs-plugins
  commit 4987918a068fcf4b3efbb584db5dd05e5f57c669
  MIT License, Copyright (c) 2026 Nous Research (LICENSE)

Rewritten: the observe-with-approval loop the plugin is — a business connection's
customer messages read from the Bot API, one draft per customer chat, the draft
rendered into the owner's DM with Send, Edit and Discard buttons, the button
presses applied (a Send delivers with the connection's business_connection_id,
an Edit makes the owner's next DM the outgoing text, a Discard drops it), the
owner-only authorization, the draft TTL checked where a draft is acted on, the
one-pending-draft rule that supersedes an older draft, the muted-chat list, the
Send button hidden without can_reply, /biz and its pause/resume/off/on
subcommands, and the shipped secretary persona. The plugin's asyncio handlers
and SQLite store became three tools (tg_business_poll, tg_business_draft,
tg_business_send), one JSON state file written 0600, and nlk:http for every
Bot API call; the model the plugin used to call for its draft is the model
reading the poll answer, so the persona travels in the poll answer.

Left out: the host plugin's asynchronous plumbing — a per-chat debounce timer,
the Python Telegram handler registrations (business_connection,
business_message, edited_business_message, callback_query, and the private-DM
edit capture at handler group -1), and the inline reply rendering helpers that
sent rather than edited where Telegram refused an edit. A cell has no such
handlers, so the model calls the tools: the draft DM is still the approval
surface, and the debounce's effect (a burst becomes one draft) survives as the
superseded status. Also left out: the SQLite schema and its migrations, which
one JSON file replaces; the plugin's Markdown status dashboard, which /biz
renders as text; and the tests' pytest fixtures, replaced by this cell's slice.
